I never thought I’d be reviewing Flight Simulator, as I’ve never really been much of a fan, so when I saw that there was an opportunity to review it I said to myself “meh, I usually play fighter flight sims, could this be good?”
So, on I went and did the download; and this is what I have to say about FSX: Steam Edition.
“If you’re expecting to be flying a fighter jet at mach 2, against an enemy you might not beat, or taking off from an aircraft carrier, feeling the jerk as you’re pulled from 0 to 100 in a matter of seconds you’d be wrong.” That’s what I would have said to you had you bought the standard edition of this game, but the complete opposite can be said for this game.
The part of this version that sticks out the most is the missions menu, and how many new missions are available to you; not to mention the new aircraft. Starting you off is the standard tutorial missions:
Taking off;
Landing;
Basic flight;
Large aircraft training;
Helicopter training;
These are just a few training missions that are available.
After a few hours of training your are sent on your first mission, to take the Secretary of Defence on a tour of Cape Canaveral. This can be a pretty difficult first mission if you consider the Secretary as a sore back (why in the hell is he flying in a twin prop plane then!?), if you are lucky, and a damn good pilot, you’ll be able to take them on the tour and return safely back at the airport. This mission should have been put in the more advanced section, rather than the very first mission you fly out of training.
As you move down the list of missions, you will come across a couple where you can use and F/A 18, this was one I personally enjoyed. In the first of these missions you are required to patrol Cape Canaveral as there is a launch happening in a couple of minutes. Your job is to keep the airspace clear of any traffic, you fly with a wingman who will order you to fly certain paths as you wait for the shuttle to launch. A little later on you encounter a Cesena flying right through the launch area, you are then asked to lead it to a nearby airstrip; perform a touch and go; and return to your original flight path. This mission again puts you in the thick of things, without giving you any idea of how to take off from a carrier; land on it again; or even how to fly a fighter jet, you need to basically learn as you fly. The controls are very sensitive, so pushing the throttle just a little, could cause you to shoot forward at a high speed. I understand that fighters are quite temperamental in reality, but I somehow dobt they are that bad. Even so there really should have been training for the F/A 18 in the tutorial section.
As you scroll down you will find training on how to do all the things necessary to fly a fighter, however most of that things you are being told don’t have any corresponding keys for the keyboard such as;
The harpoon lock. What key do you press to activate it?. It took me 30 minutes to find it in the cockpit, and I couldn’t find it in the key bindings;
The arrester hook. After trying to find the harpoon lock I decided not to even bother with this one;
It’s not all that bad though, you do get the opportunity to chase a U.F.O. while trying to upload a virus into their systems (Remind you of a movie?) You can also use the F/A 18 in free flight, so if you want you can create a sonic boom over residential areas; you can even rebase your fighter to different airbases around the world.
Being from South Africa, it was also hard for me to fly around my own province, and try to find the landmarks that stand out; like the Johannesburg sky-line, this is one of the most iconic symbols of the province and it wasn’t even there. We do have more than just a few houses and bush in the city.
Thinking this might just be a tiny error on the designers part, I decided to take a flight out of Cape Town. As I took off it seemed to be alright. I even found Robben Island, but again there were no landmarks, except of course Table Mountain, although and I say this with the utmost sincerity to the people who haven’t visited South Africa, there are no houses on top of table mountain. I’m sure South Africa isn’t the only country to be forgotten in this game, sadly I haven’t had the chance to look around to see.
That being said that game does still have appeal. Once you get past the problems encountered you will want to fly more; especially in free flight mode, where you can plan your own flight from anywhere in the world.
The graphics have taken a big turn since the last time I played Flight Simulator; you can now see the cars and trucks on the road; the sunlight as you’re flying over the ocean is consistent; and glare is a lot more noticeable than it was in previous versions.
Bearing in mind I did use a joystick to fly, the controls are easier to handle, (in most planes).
So if you are a pilot at heart and you have a need to take to the sky, this game will give you what you need, if however you are looking for a fast paced game the missions will fill your need for a short while, until you get to a point where you can’t handle not being able to complete a mission, that you just turn off and move to a new game. This one is definitely for the hardcore fans.

I almost never bother to write recommendations but LiS is special. It was a unique experience, it moved me emotionally like no game has ever done before in over 20 years of gaming. It's now almost a month since I played the game and I still feel warm inside thinking back on it. I really recommend going into the game without any prior knowledge though, so avoid trailers beyond episode 1. This review shouldn't spoil anything.
Notes:
Playing with a controller is highly recommended!
Upsides:
+ The first episode is available for FREE!
+ Uniquely emotional, video game transcending experience;
+ Artistically beautiful, walking through parts of Arcadia Bay just felt cozy at times;
+ Good use of licensed Music.
Downsides:
- Gameplay may be a bit lacking, I suppose it plays more like an interactive film. I thought it was interesting because you got sort off the experience of watching 5 good feature films while the fact that you're playing it increased the engagement with the story, moral choices and their consequences;
- I suppose the graphics won't push your GPU to its limits. But frankly, I didn't care, artistically the game looked beautiful;
- Replay value is limited. Since you know more or less what's going to happen, no replay will ever feel the same as the first playthrough. Doesn't mean that this first time is worth it though.
Try Life is Strange. It's a wonderfull experience. I recommend going into it as a blank slate, avoid watching trailers or discussions. Episode 1 is available free. So, there's no excuse. You won't regret it!

